<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Callicratidas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/406-bce">406 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Callicratidas was a Spartan navarch, or fleet commander, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a>, who rose to prominence although he belonged to the non-citizen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mothax">mothax</a> class. In 406 BCE he was sent to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> to take over the Spartan fleet from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysander">Lysander</a>, who had apparently returned the money meant to pay it to the Persian prince Cyrus. As a traditionalist Spartan he was loath to ask a Persian for funds, and the city of Miletus eventually paid instead. After a first victory over the Athenian admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conon">Conon</a> at Mytilene, he was defeated and killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-arginusae">Battle of Arginusae</a> in 406 BCE. He opposed alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and said he would seek peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, and was well regarded after his death.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ancient writers name as mothakes Lysander, who destroyed the Athenian fleet at the Battle of Aegospotami in 405 BCE and blockaded Athens into surrender, Gylippus, who organised the defence of Syracuse and destroyed the Athenian expedition in 413 BCE, and <b>Callicratidas</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/sparta">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Sparta</a></li></ul></section>
